# .config/forbidden-names — TEMPLATE. Copy it, do not edit it in place.
#
#     mkdir -p .config
#     cp example/forbidden-names.example .config/forbidden-names
#
# Why this file is a template and the list itself is not tracked
# -------------------------------------------------------------
# This list exists to keep certain proper names out of this repository. A
# blacklist cannot be committed: writing a name down in order to check for
# it publishes the name, which is the exact thing the list exists to
# prevent. So the working copy lives at .config/forbidden-names, which
# .gitignore excludes ("Local configuration — operator-owned, never
# committed").
#
# THIS FILE MUST NEVER BE COMMITTED either, once it carries a real name.
# The template below is safe to track only because every entry in it is a
# placeholder that will never match anything.
#
# Format
# ------
# One entry per line.
#
#   * Blank lines are ignored.
#   * A line whose first non-space character is '#' is a comment.
#   * Everything else is a needle.
#
# A plain needle is matched as a case-insensitive SUBSTRING of each line —
# not as a whole word, and not with any boundary rule. Substring is
# deliberate: a boundary rule built on [A-Za-z0-9._-] treats a hyphen as a
# word character, so it cannot match a name glued to a prefix
# ("lib" + NAME + "_rs.dylib") nor a name inside a plan-id slug
# ("2026xxxx-" + NAME + "-step"). Those are the two forms that actually
# leak, and a rule that cannot match them is green over exactly what it was
# written for. False positives are the right way round here: a two-second
# rewrite beats a permanent publication.
#
# There is no per-file allowlist and no tests/ exemption. A private name is
# as published inside a fixture as inside a secret.
#
# Regular expressions
# -------------------
# Prefix an entry with "re:" to match it as a case-insensitive regular
# expression instead of a literal. Use this for a *shape* — something where
# the structure matters rather than any one word in it — for example a live
# quote identifier. Python's `re` syntax is what is used, not POSIX ERE, and
# a pattern that does not compile is a hard error rather than a silently
# skipped entry: an uncompilable pattern means the check is not running as
# you configured it, and that must not read as a pass.
#
#     re:<some-shape>[0-9]{<n>}
#
# A line that is exactly "re:" with nothing after it is an error too.
#
# Duplicates
# ----------
# An entry repeated twice is dropped with a warning rather than reported
# twice, so the violation count you see is the number of *distinct* needles
# that fired. Note that substring matching makes shorter entries subsume
# longer ones: if you list "widget" you do not need "widget-loader", though
# keeping both costs nothing but a second line on the same finding.
#
# When the list is missing or has no usable entry
# -----------------------------------------------
# The pre-commit and pre-push hooks exit 2 — an explicit "the check did not
# run" — rather than 0. An absent list and a clean tree have to be
# distinguishable, because a gate that silently stops checking is
# indistinguishable from a gate that is passing. That is not a
# hypothetical: this repository once had a name check driven by an
# environment variable that was never set on any machine, and it reported a
# pass forever while checking nothing.
#
# The checks that read this file
# ------------------------------
#   * scripts/pre_commit_gate.sh  -> scan_forbidden_names.sh --index
#   * scripts/scan_commit_range.sh -> scan_forbidden_names.sh --range ...
#
# Both run the scanner at scripts/scanners/scan_forbidden_names.py, which is
# where the matching lives; nothing here is enforced by pytest, on purpose —
# by the time the suite is green the text is already in a commit.

# --- literals ---------------------------------------------------------------
# One real private proper name per line, verbatim, as it appears in a leak.
#
# <a-private-project-name>
# <a-private-feature-branch>
# <a-private-vocabulary-term>

# --- shapes -----------------------------------------------------------------
# re:<pattern> for structure rather than a specific word.
#
# re:symbol=[0-9]{6}
